# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-16, panel 069 — `==` gave two answers for the same
# contents, and the shortcut that did it was not about floats.
#
# SYMPTOM: with `n = 0.0 / 0.0`, `a = [n]`, `b = a` and `c = [n]`, the program
# printed `a == b` TRUE and `a == c` FALSE. Identical contents, two answers,
# decided by how the second value happened to be produced. Exit 0, no
# diagnostic, no abort. `{str: f64}` did the same through its own copy of the
# line, reachable through a nan VALUE — a nan KEY aborts and cannot get there,
# which is why that second site looked unreachable and survived the first audit.
#
# CAUSE: `runtime/parts/array.c:112` and `runtime/parts/map.c:201` each carried
# an uncommented `if (a == b) return true;`. Copy-on-write makes `b = a` share a
# header, so the shortcut fired for the copy and the elementwise walk ran for the
# rebuild. That made copy-on-write's sharing observable, and `spec:73` promises
# "no aliasing exists anywhere".
#
# WHY IT IS NOT A FLOAT BUG, which is the finding the sitting turned on: the
# compiler-engineer reproduced it with NO FLOAT IN THE PROGRAM. With panel 061's
# partial-record refusal reachable, the shared operands returned `true` at exit 0
# while the distinct ones fired the deliberate `hero_panic` that refusal exists
# to produce. An optimisation that silently defeats a loud refusal is CLAUDE.md
# §11's rule inverted — and there was not even a comment on the line, which is
# why nobody had re-read it.
#
# WHY THE SHORTCUT'S USUAL DEFENCES DO NOT REACH HERE, both measured:
#   - SPEED. CLAUDE.md §13 forbids it as a justification, and it was not even
#     true: the golden harness got ~3% FASTER without the line.
#   - TERMINATION on a cyclic value. That is Python's reason and it needs cycles.
#     design.md:2855 makes them unconstructible — "if values are never aliased,
#     reference cycles cannot be constructed" — verified two ways in the sitting:
#     pushing a record into its own array field yields a snapshot whose own
#     children are empty, and mutating the parent afterwards does not reach it.
#
# THE PRECEDENT, and it is why this case exists rather than a comment: Swift
# ships this exact bug — COW arrays, a `_buffer.identity` fast path — filed as
# SR-6181 in October 2017 and still open, and swift-foundation ADDED the same
# fast path to `Data` in May 2025. No language has removed a container-level one.
# A defect nobody else fixed is a defect that comes back unless something fires.
# Date: 2026-08-16.

function main()
    n = 0.0 / 0.0

    # The four lines the defect lived on. All four must agree, and after the
    # repair they agree on `false` — which is IEEE's answer and, more to the
    # point, the SAME answer whichever way the second value was made.
    a = [n]
    b = a
    c = [n]
    print(a == b, " ", a == c)

    ma: {str: f64} @ {"k": n}
    mb = ma
    mc: {str: f64} @ {"k": n}
    print(ma == mb, " ", ma == mc)

    # And the whole point of the repair is that ORDINARY equality is untouched:
    # a copy of a value holding no nan is still equal to its source, and a
    # rebuilt twin still equals it too. If either of these flips, the fix went
    # too far and every program in the corpus is wrong.
    xs = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
    ys = xs
    zs = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
    print(xs == ys, " ", xs == zs)

    ns: {str: i64} @ {"a": 1}
    os = ns
    ps: {str: i64} @ {"a": 1}
    print(ns == os, " ", ns == ps)

    # The length check that sits above the deleted line stays, because a length
    # is a fact about the contents and not about an address.
    print([1.0] == [1.0, 2.0])

# Since 2026-09-10 the specification's sections are numbered and a citation names
# the section, never a line (CL-037). The line numbers above were written against
# the layout at commit 834d804f; read today they are:
#   spec:73 is spec § 3 Types` promises
